UI sends the representation to create a permission of a set of users

{
  "name": "My Users Permission",
  "type": "scope",
  "resourceType": "Users",
  "resources": [
      "vlasta",
      "pedro",
      "martin",
      "stefan"
    ],
    "scopes": [
      "manage",
      "map-roles"
    ],
    "policies": [
      "mypolicy1",
      "mypolicy2"
    ]
}

UI sends the representation to create a permission of a all users

{
  "name": "My Users Permission",
  "type": "scope",
  "resourceType": "Users",
  "scopes": [
    "manage",
    "map-roles"
  ],
  "policies": [
    "mypolicy1",
    "mypolicy2"
  ]
}


The backend does the following whenever updating the admin-permission client:

* Run validations based on the schema
* For each resource in the payload, it will create a resource object and associated with this objects the scopes in the payload
** For each resource object we will be adding an attribute to reference a "searchable" attribute like the ID, username, and firstname
* Create a single scope-based permission with the resources previously created, the scopes in the payload, and the policies in the payload

The user wants to find a permission for a specific resource:

* select * from policy p where p.type = 'scope' and p.resources in (select r.id from resource r where r.type = 'Users' and r.attributes.key == 'vlasta')
* select * from policy p where p.type = 'scope' and p.resources in (select r.id from resource r where r.type = 'Users' and r.searcheableField == 'vlasta')
* select * from policy p where p.type = 'scope' and p.resources in (select r.id from resource r where r.type = 'Cliemt' and r.searcheableField == 'myclientid')





